diff --git a/chapters/dxr-foundations/Starter/DxrLab.hlsl b/chapters/dxr-foundations/Starter/DxrLab.hlsl
--- a/chapters/dxr-foundations/Starter/DxrLab.hlsl
+++ b/chapters/dxr-foundations/Starter/DxrLab.hlsl
@@ -1,25 +1,20 @@
-// Chapter 33 checkpoint 1: the traversal answers the ray, and the library reports only that.
+// Chapter 33 checkpoint 2: the closest-hit shader reports the identity the traversal resolved.
 //
-// The lab becomes the Solution variant here, so the renderer stops compiling this file as a `cs_6_0` compute
-// shader and starts compiling it at runtime as a `lib_6_3` DXIL library. Around that library it now builds a
-// one-geometry bottom-level structure, packs three row-major 3x4 instances and builds a top-level structure over
-// them, resolves the library into a raytracing state object with a global root signature and a hit-group-local
-// root signature, records a shader table of one ray generation record, two miss records and four hit-group
-// records, and submits `SetPipelineState1` and `DispatchRays`. Nine ordered stages replace the Starter's three,
-// and the stage list, the ledger, the recorded barriers and the prebuild report in the readback are the frame's
-// own account of that.
+// A hit is not a boolean. The traversal that found it already knows which instance of the top-level structure it
+// belongs to, which identifier that instance carries, which triangle of the bottom-level geometry it is, and
+// which side of that triangle the ray arrived from, and DXR exposes each of those to the closest-hit shader as an
+// intrinsic rather than as something the shader searches for.
 //
-// The library is deliberately at its beginning. It declares the payload the state object sizes, forms the same
-// primary ray the Starter formed, hands it to a single `TraceRay`, and publishes what came back:
+//   * `InstanceIndex()` is the instance's position in the top-level structure and `InstanceID()` is the 24-bit
+//     value its instance description carries. This lab deliberately gives the two different values, so a record
+//     that reports both proves which of them a reader is looking at.
+//   * `PrimitiveIndex()` is the triangle's index inside its geometry, which is the only way to see that one
+//     bottom-level structure holds more than one triangle.
+//   * `HitKind()` is the object-space winding test D3D12 specifies: `HIT_KIND_TRIANGLE_FRONT_FACE` for a triangle
+//     whose vertices wind clockwise as seen from the ray, and `HIT_KIND_TRIANGLE_BACK_FACE` otherwise. It is
+//     decided from object-space vertices and the transformed ray; this scene's x mirror therefore preserves it.
 //
-//   * `ClosestHitMain` reports that it ran and that the ray hit a triangle. It reports nothing else yet, so every
-//     hit still publishes an invalid instance, primitive and record index, a distance of zero and no colour.
-//   * `MissBackground` and `MissVoid` are complete. Each reports the index of the record it occupies, which is
-//     what separates "a miss shader ran" from "the miss record this dispatch selected ran".
-//
-// The identity the traversal resolved, the geometry of the hit, the record it selected and the terms the
-// configuration owns each arrive in a later checkpoint, and every field none of them has written yet reads back
-// as the invalid value the ray generation shader put there.
+// The distance, the barycentrics, the record the traversal selected and the colour it carries are still missing.

 #include "../Common/DxrShared.hlsli"

@@ -99,7 +94,13 @@ struct LabPayload
 [shader("closesthit")] void ClosestHitMain(inout LabPayload payload,
                                            in BuiltInTriangleIntersectionAttributes attributes)
 {
-    payload.status |= StatusHit | StatusClosestHitRan;
+    bool frontFace = HitKind() == HIT_KIND_TRIANGLE_FRONT_FACE;
+    payload.status |= StatusHit | StatusClosestHitRan | (frontFace ? StatusFrontFace : StatusBackFace);
+    payload.instanceIndex = InstanceIndex();
+    payload.instanceId = InstanceID();
+    payload.primitiveIndex = PrimitiveIndex();
+    payload.hitKind = HitKind();
+    payload.missShaderIndex = InvalidIndex;
 }

